<script>
import { url } from '@abide/abide'
import Doc from '#src/docs/Doc.abide'
import Example from '#src/docs/Example.abide'
import Section from '#src/docs/Section.abide'
import Contacts from './Contacts.abide'
import ContactsAndQueue from './ContactsAndQueue.abide'
import contactsSource from './Contacts.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import contactsAndQueueSource from './ContactsAndQueue.abide' with { type: 'text' }
</script>

<Doc>
    <Section title="One list block">
        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>You write</th><th>Over</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>{'{#for item, index of list by key}'}</code></td><td>a collection you hold</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>{'{#for item of iterable}'}</code></td><td>an async iterable, as it produces</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p><code>index</code> and <code>by</code> are both optional, and the second is the one worth
        spelling. The subject is read like any other name, so <code>{'{#for row of rows}'}</code>
        walks the array <code>rows</code> holds, and walks it again each time that array changes — a
        value a followed <code>GET</code> keeps current included.</p>

        <p>It iterates <strong>zero times</strong> over an absent subject. A list still loading is
        not an error and not a special case; it is a block with nothing in it yet.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A key is what makes a reorder a reorder">
        <Example
            id="list-keyed"
            title={'`{#for row of rows by row.id}` — a reorder moves nodes'}
            summary="Three contacts. Swap the top two, then reverse the lot."
            files={{
                'Contacts.abide': contactsSource,
            }}
        >
            <Contacts />
        </Example>

        <p><em>1 clause, 0 tables</em> — the arm's key-to-node map is what <code>by</code> says in
        four characters.</p>

        <p>Without <code>by</code>, a <code>{'{#for}'}</code> is <strong>positional</strong>: item
        one is the first node, item two the second, and a list whose order changed is a list where
        every node's content changed. The output is identical, which is exactly the problem — a
        rebuild renders correctly and throws away the focus, the selection, the scroll position and
        any transition that was mid-flight.</p>

        <p>That is why the block <strong>warns in development</strong> where it has no key and a
        stateful body. A body holding an input or a component instance is a body where the
        difference is visible, and the warning is the only moment anything says so.</p>

        <p>A key is any expression, and the honest one is whatever the row is identified by in the
        system it came from. <code>by row.id</code> and <code>by row</code> differ only in whether
        identity survives a re-fetch.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/values/patch-a-list-from-a-live-feed')}>Patching a list</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A value can carry its own key">
        <Example
            id="list-by"
            title={'`reactive(contacts, { by: \'id\' })` — every list over it moves nodes'}
            summary="The contacts and the call queue, one list shown twice. Swap the top two."
            files={{
                'ContactsAndQueue.abide': contactsAndQueueSource,
            }}
        >
            <ContactsAndQueue />
        </Example>

        <p><em>1 option, 2 tables</em> — the arm keeps a key-to-node map per list, and <code>by:
        'id'</code> on the value is both of them.</p>

        <p>Where one list is rendered in more than one place, the key belongs to the value rather
        than to each block that walks it. <code>by</code> on the <code>reactive</code> names what
        tells two elements apart — a property, or a function of the element — and a
        <code>{'{#for}'}</code> that writes no key of its own takes it.</p>

        <p>It reaches a block whose subject is the value <strong>named alone</strong>.
        <code>{'{#for c of contacts.slice(0, 3)}'}</code> walks a new array computed from the value,
        which declared no key, so that block is positional again and writes <code>by c.id</code>
        itself. A key the block writes wins over the value's.</p>

        <p><code>by</code> is not <code>identity</code>. <code>identity</code> decides whether the
        whole value changed, and <code>by</code> decides which row is which once it has.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Two cases catch two different mistakes">
        <p>A <strong>two-row swap</strong> is what tells a minimal reorder from a rebuild: both
        render the same names, and only one of them moved two nodes to do it. A <strong>full
        reverse</strong> cannot — every position changed, so a rebuild and a reorder are
        indistinguishable by cost.</p>

        <p>The reverse earns its place on the other question. A transposition that gets the ends
        right and the middle wrong passes a swap and fails a reverse, so the case that is useless
        for pricing is the one that catches the bug. Both, or neither is worth running.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/values/show-a-value-that-isnt-there-yet')}>Loading states</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Over an async iterable, the same block appends">
        <p>A subject that is an async iterable and not also iterable — an async generator, say —
        produces over time, and the block appends each item as it lands rather than waiting for the
        last, reconciling nothing (32.15).</p>

        <p>A channel is not that case. It is a <code>reactive</code> holding an array, so
        <code>{'{#for m of room}'}</code> reads the array and reconciles it as messages are appended
        and the front is trimmed. With <code>by: 'id'</code> on the value, each message moves one
        row (32.32); without it the rows are positional, and trimming the front rebuilds every
        one.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/data/send-data-as-it-arrives')}>Streaming data</a> ·
        <a href={url('/values/let-anything-publish-and-anything-read')}>Channels</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Next">
        <ul>
            <li><a href={url('/templates/show-markup-conditionally')}>Conditionals</a> — the block
            for one rather than many</li>
            <li><a href={url('/templates/reuse-a-piece-of-markup')}>Components</a> — a row that is
            worth its own file</li>
            <li><a href={url('/values/patch-a-list-from-a-live-feed')}>Patching a list</a> —
            changing one row of many</li>
        </ul>
    </Section>
</Doc>
